agent agent   4314 Nov 11 14:08 reference_query_plan.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  83289 Nov 11 14:08 reference_query_plan_agent_claude_20261111_140816.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  20184 Nov 11 14:08 reference_query_plan_prompt.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Nov 11 14:08 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    280 Nov 11 14:08 reference_query_plan_validation_feedback.md\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3726 Nov 11 14:10 reference_retrieval.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   7704 Nov 11 14:10 references.bib\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  24107 Nov 11 14:10 references_provenance.json\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Nov 11 14:08 results -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    266 Nov 11 14:12 strategy_validation_feedback.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    272 Nov 11 14:12 strategy_validation_feedback.md\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent      7 Nov 11 14:08 task -> ../task\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n# Paper strategy (paper_standalone, ICLR 2026 single-column, 8-9 body pages)\n\n**Status:** ready.\n\n## Provisional title\nIs Grokking Delay Width-Dependent? A Censoring-Aware Seed-Cluster Slope Test on Modular Addition Finds No Resolvable Trend\n\n## Central question\nIn a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW on modular addition (p=97, train fraction 0.4, weight decay 1.0, full batch, 10,000-step limit), does the delay between fitting the training set and generalizing decrease with width, as a pre-declared seed-cluster slope test can detect?\n\n## Central thesis\nThe sealed final evaluation (outcome `qualified`, status completed, correct true; 4 seeds x 5 widths 16-256) measured a ln(delay)-vs-log2(width) slope of -0.020 with 95% seed-cluster bootstrap CI [-0.585, 0.354]. Under the pre-declared rule (CI entirely below zero = supported; at/above zero = refuted; straddling = inconclusive) the verdict is **inconclusive**. 40% of runs were censored at the step limit. The paper's contribution is a calibrated inconclusive measurement, a censoring-aware test design with explicit resolution limits, and an inventory of what the evidence cannot say.\n\n\"Qualified\" means the evaluation ran validly. It does not mean the width hypothesis was supported. Gate diagnostics beyond `final.qualification.*` are unknown and must be stated as such.\n\n## Why this framing (and not the others)\n- Capacity-crossover story (t_mem vs t_gen, critical width vs train fraction): no such results exist in the artifacts. Rejected.\n- Lazy-to-rich / effective-learning-rate confound explanation: the hidden-exponent sweep script was written but never executed, so no comparison across reparametrization strengths exists. The frozen model contains the reparametrization, but its effect is not isolated. Listed as untested.\n- \"Best search score\" story: scores come from a different data plane, 2 seeds each, with an undisclosed score formula, and ordering is not monotone with censoring. Descriptive context only.\n- No downstream_study block, no edit context: fresh framing; the prior-story status is `rejected` only in the sense that nothing is carried over.\n\n## Method groups\n1. Frozen evaluated model variant (proposed): one-layer transformer, last-position forward, init scale 0.5, reference width 128, output exponent 0.5, hidden reparametrization exponent 0.5.\n2. Evaluator-owned reference trainer (baseline): single-seed slope -0.368, inside the CI; no significance claim, and no attribution of the gap to the reparametrization.\n3. Control episodes (supporting): control max validation accuracy 0.0122; missing episodes 0.\n4. Search-phase variants (descriptive ablation-like context): scores 0.798 / 0.828 / 0.850, censored fractions 0.667 / 1.0 / 0.667, 2 seeds each. Kept separate from final.\n\n## Results focus\n- Slope, CI and verdict, with the reference slope for cross-check.\n- Resolution analysis (derived from manifest values): delay is floored at 250 and capped at 10,000 steps, so |slope| over 4 width doublings is at most ln(40)/4 ≈ 0.92. The CI (width ≈ 0.94) covers much of that range. With 4 seed clusters the bootstrap is coarse.\n- Censoring (0.4) and validity checks.\n- Seed-level spread of the evaluator summary score (0.607, 0.733, 0.717, 0.860), described as variability, not performance.\n- Explicitly unavailable: per-width/per-seed delays, t_mem/t_gen, d*(f), train-fraction sweeps, hidden-exponent sweep, weight-decay control.\n\n## Figures\n1. `protocol_overview` (required, full width, early placement, concept figure via generate-concept-figure): delay definition, censoring, slope test, three-way verdict, this run's outcome marked.\n2. `slope_interval_vs_decision_rule` (required, matplotlib, ~0.6 text width, 1 panel): CI, reference point, zero line, decision regions, attainable-slope bound.\n3. `search_variants_descriptive` (optional, supplement-friendly, full width, 2 panels x 3 categories): labelled \"search phase, 2 seeds, not final\".\nAll figures: export PDF + 300 DPI PNG, reference the PDF in LaTeX, no upscaled rasters.\n\n## Body-page use\nIntro ~1.2 pp; related work ~0.8; protocol + frozen model ~2; results ~2 (including the resolution analysis); discussion of alternatives (censoring ceiling, 4 seeds, reparametrization confound, single operation/prime) ~1; limitations/scope ~0.7. No padding. If short, deepen the resolution analysis and move the seed-level table into the main text.\n\n## Supplement\nFull protocol/hyperparameters; frozen-model constants; seed-level episode table; descriptive search table; untested hypotheses and unexecuted analyses; LLM usage statement (required by venue).\n\n## Avoid\nHeadings: search campaign, milestones, progression, cycles, first successful run, Experiment A/B, run log.\nPhrases: correctness rate, correct and eligible, combined_score, rubric, winner, any directional width claim, \"not qualified\", \"qualified therefore the hypothesis holds\".\n\n## Citations\nOnly keys in `references.bib` that are relevant: Power2022GrokkingGB, Nanda2023ProgressMF, Liu2022OmnigrokGB, Kumar2023GrokkingAT, Huang2024UnifiedVO; Lyle2024NormalizationAE and Nam2024DecouplingDR only if described accurately. Other starter references (circuit efficiency, double descent, AdamW, etc.) are not in the bib and must not be cited unless added. Unrelated bib entries must not be cited.\nfigures:\n\nresults:\n_idea_generation\nattempts_history.jsonl\nbest_program_manifest.json\nconclusions.md\nconclusions_history.jsonl\nconclusions_other_workers.md\nevents.jsonl\nidea_generation_failures.jsonl\nideas\nideas_history.jsonl\nlatest_run_manifest.json\nmemory.jsonl\nmemory.md\nouter_state.json\ntask","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dd35f6f-91b7-4850-b942-7d959e7db0b3","uuid":"93c5bf08-ca07-4276-80d2-9ff56c99a2dc","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:12:18.429Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"total 684\ndrwx------ 5 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 14:12 .\ndrwx------ 8 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 14:08 ..\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    751 Nov 11 14:08 .agentic_paper_workspace.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 14:08 .claude\nprw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 14:12 .paper_stream_20261111_140816.fifo\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 14:08 .paper_two_stage_enabled\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3685 Nov 11 14:12 VENUE.md\ndrwx------ 2 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 14:08 figures\nlrwxrwxrwx 1 agent agent     16 Nov 11 14:08 global_memory -> ../investigation\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   6541 Nov 11 14:08 global_memory_recent.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   3142 Nov 11 14:08 global_memory_summary.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent    955 Nov 11 14:12 memory.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  26973 Nov 10 13:47 paper.bst\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent   8934 Nov 10 13:47 paper.sty\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 14:12 paper_agent_claude_20261111_140816.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  31945 Nov 11 14:08 paper_data.json\ndrwx------ 3 agent agent   4096 Nov 11 14:08 paper_diagnostics\n-rw------- 1 agent agent      0 Nov 11 14:08 paper_diagnostics_20261111_140816_47164.log\n-rw------- 1 agent agent   2526 Nov 11 14:12 paper_events_20261111_140816.jsonl\n-rw------- 1 agent agent    795 Nov 11 14:08 paper_prep_20261111_140816.json\n-rw------- 1 agent agent  54977 Nov 11 14:12 paper_prompt.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 agent agent  18219