{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwHRWfn2R4vsVQzbo3cv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01R7UMwVXZaCQrBF4k1hp9NU","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\newcommand{\\ln}{\\operatorname{ln}}\n\n\\title{Does Grokking Delay Depend on Width? An Inconclusive, Censoring-Aware Slope Test on Modular Addition}\n\\author{Anonymous authors}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nDelayed generalization (``grokking'') on modular arithmetic is explained through memorization capacity, weight norm, circuit efficiency, or a lazy-to-rich transition, but its dependence on model size has received little systematic measurement.\nWe test one falsifiable prediction in a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW (weight decay $1.0$, full batch) on modular addition modulo $97$: that the delay between fitting the training pairs and generalizing to held-out pairs shrinks as width grows.\nDelay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\nThe measured slope is $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$, so the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}; $40\\%$ of the $20$ runs were censored, and the interval covers about half of the slopes that the delay floor and cap permit.\nA single-seed reference trainer gives $-0.368$, inside the interval.\nThe evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a calibrated non-answer rather than a failure; we state what it cannot say and which measurements would resolve it.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\nIn small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the training split almost immediately and generalize to held-out pairs only after much longer training \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. Several accounts of this delay have been proposed: a mechanistic account in which a generalizing circuit forms gradually under weight decay \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}, a loss-landscape and weight-norm account \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}, a circuit-efficiency account \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}, and a lazy-to-rich account of training dynamics \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}. How the delay depends on \\emph{model size} is a more practical question: it constrains these accounts and informs which model sizes are worth using in experiments on the phenomenon. A capacity-based reading predicts that wider models memorize sooner, and, if generalization time is less sensitive to width, that the delay changes systematically with width.\n\nThis paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, and step limit, and the width $d$ is varied over $\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$. The prediction under test is that the delay decreases with width, operationalized as the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2 d$ being negative with a 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval entirely below zero. The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is \\emph{refuted} if the interval lies at or above zero and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}).\n\nThe outcome is inconclusive. The sealed evaluation, run on four seeds at each of five widths, gives a slope of $-0.020$ with 95\\% interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$. The evaluation was valid in the sense that it completed and passed the evaluator's validity checks (the evaluator labels it \\texttt{qualified}); this status concerns the integrity of the measurement and does not mean that the width prediction was supported. Because a flat answer is easy to misread, much of this paper is about \\emph{why} the measurement is inconclusive and how large an effect it could have resolved.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome with its uncertainty and its cross-check against a single-seed reference trainer (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). (iii) We derive, from the protocol constants and the reported interval, the resolution limits of this design: the range of slopes the floor and cap allow, the fraction of that range covered by the interval, and the coarseness of a four-cluster bootstrap (Section~\\ref{sec:resolution}). (iv) We list what the retained artifacts cannot support, including per-width delays, a mechanistic decomposition, and any isolation of the evaluated model's width-dependent parametrization (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}). We make no claim that delay is, or is not, width-dependent in modular addition.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Overview of the width-dependence test. Delay is the gap between the first checkpoint at which training accuracy reaches $0.99$ and the first at which held-out accuracy does, floored at one probe interval and set to the step limit if either threshold is never reached. The slope of $\\ln$(delay) against $\\log_2$(width) is estimated over five widths and four seeds, with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval; the interval, not the point estimate, determines the verdict. In this study the interval straddles zero and the verdict is inconclusive.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} reverse-engineer one-layer transformers trained on modular addition and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. The present study does not test them against each other.\n\n\\paragraph{Model size, memorization, and double descent.} Test error can vary non-monotonically with model size and training time \\citep{Nakkiran2019DeepDD}; networks can fit random labels, so fitting the training set does not by itself imply generalization \\citep{Zhang2016UnderstandingDL}; memorization has been studied empirically during language-model training \\citep{Tirumala2022MemorizationWO} and its information content estimated per model \\citep{Morris2025HowMD}. These works motivate a capacity-based reading of the grokking delay, in which fitting time and generalization time depend differently on width. We use them only as motivation: the artifacts of this study do not record fitting time and generalization time separately.\n\n\\paragraph{Parametrization and optimizer scale.} The evaluated model contains width-dependent multipliers on hidden and output layers (Section~\\ref{sec:model}), motivated by the observation that the step size a layer effectively receives under an adaptive optimizer depends on its parametrization. The role of effective learning rates under normalization has been studied in reinforcement learning \\citep{Lyle2024NormalizationAE}, practical measures of dynamical richness have been proposed \\citep{Nam2024DecouplingDR}, and decoupled weight decay, which we use, is introduced by \\citet{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}. We do not measure the effect of these multipliers in isolation.\n\n\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Problem setup and hypothesis}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n\\paragraph{Task.} Inputs are all $p^2=9{,}409$ ordered pairs $(a,b)$ with $a,b\\in\\{0,\\dots,96\\}$ and label $(a+b)\\bmod 97$. For each seed, a random $40\\%$ of pairs ($3{,}763$) forms the training split and the remaining $5{,}646$ pairs are held out; the evaluator, not the model, owns the split, the checkpoint schedule, and all metrics.\n\n\\paragraph{Delay.} Training runs for at most $T=10{,}000$ full-batch steps and is probed every $250$ steps, giving $40$ checkpoints at which accuracy is computed by the evaluator on all pairs. Let $t_{\\mathrm{fit}}$ be the first checkpoint at which training accuracy is at least $0.99$, and $t_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ the first at which held-out accuracy is at least $0.99$. The delay is\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta = \\begin{cases} \\max(t_{\\mathrm{gen}}-t_{\\mathrm{fit}},\\,250) & \\text{if both thresholds are reached,}\\\\ 10{,}000 & \\text{otherwise (censored).}\\end{cases}\n\\end{equation}\nA run is censored if \\emph{either} threshold is never reached. Censored runs keep the cap value and are never dropped or reweighted. The floor of one probe interval ($250$ steps) also applies when held-out accuracy crosses $0.99$ no later than training accuracy does. Both the floor and the cap therefore bound $\\ln\\Delta$ to $[\\ln 250,\\ln 10{,}000]$, a ratio of $40$.\n\n\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n\n\\subsection{Evaluated model}\n\\label{sec:model}\nThe evaluated configuration is a one-layer, four-head transformer with width $d$ (the model dimension), an MLP of hidden size $4d$ with ReLU, no normalization layers, a learned embedding for the $97$ residues plus a separator token ``$=$'' appended as the third input position, and a learned positional embedding. Attention is non-causal and only the representation at the final position is read out; the implementation computes queries, residual stream, and MLP for that position only, which by inspection of the one-layer architecture gives the same outputs as processing all positions. The parameter count is $12d^2+(2p+9)d$, from $6{,}320$ at $d=16$ to $838{,}400$ at $d=256$, within the $10^6$ budget (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-model}).\n\nRelative to the evaluator's stock model, the configuration has three modifications, all visible in the source (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_model/program.py}):\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\emph{Smaller initial weights.} All hidden and output weight matrices are multiplied by $0.5$ at initialization.\n\\item \\emph{Width-aware output scaling.} The unembedding weights are additionally halved at initialization, and the logits are multiplied by $m_d=(128/d)^{1/2}$.\n\\item \\emph{Hidden-layer reparametrization.} Each hidden matrix is written $W=m_d V$ with the same multiplier $m_d$, and $V$ is initialized as $W_{\\mathrm{init}}/m_d$ so that the function at initialization is unchanged. The optimizer updates $V$, and decoupled weight decay acts on $V$. The code comments state the intent: under Adam-type updates $V$ moves at about the learning rate per step, so $W$ moves at about $m_d$ times the learning rate, which is meant to compensate for width in the effective step size.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThe multiplier is $2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71$ at $d=16,\\dots,256$. The exponents ($0.5$) and the reference width ($128$) are fixed constants chosen before the final evaluation, not tuned on it. The training workload is dictated by the evaluator: AdamW with learning rate $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, decoupled weight decay $1.0$, full-batch updates on all $3{,}763$ training pairs, and $10{,}000$ steps. We refer to this configuration as the \\emph{evaluated model}.\n\n\\subsection{Reference trainer, controls, and validity}\n\\label{sec:controls}\n\\paragraph{Reference trainer.} The evaluator also trains its own stock transformer with the same optimizer settings and no modifications (no initialization shrinkage, no multipliers, all positions processed), using the first final seed ($201$) at each of the five widths. The OLS slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ over these five single-seed delays is the reference slope. It has no interval.\n\n\\paragraph{Controls.} Two control runs at width $64$ and seed $201$ check that the measurement does not manufacture generalization: one trains on training labels that have been permuted (at least $90\\%$ of labels changed) and one performs zero optimizer steps. A control fails if its held-out accuracy ever exceeds $0.10$; chance level for $97$ classes is about $0.010$.\n\n\\paragraph{Validity.} The evaluator marks the final evaluation valid (\\texttt{qualified}, status \\texttt{completed}) when, according to its source, all episodes completed, the compute and call budgets were respected, the controls passed, and at least one run fit the training data to $0.99$. Only these three status fields are retained in the artifacts; per-condition diagnostics beyond them are not, so we treat any finer breakdown as unknown. Diagnostics reported by the evaluated model itself are untrusted and not used: every accuracy, delay, control, and statistic below is computed by the evaluator from returned predictions.\n\n\\subsection{Scope of the retained evidence}\n\\label{sec:scope}\nThe retained final artifacts consist of the aggregate metrics listed in Section~\\ref{sec:results} and one record per seed (mean validation-accuracy score and wall-clock time). \\emph{Per-width and per-seed delays, fitting and generalization steps, and per-width censoring flags are not retained}, so we cannot show a delay-versus-width plot, and we do not infer one. Separately, a development evaluation on a different data plane (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, widths $32,64,128$, $6{,}000$ steps, two seeds) was used when configuring the evaluated model; its results are reported only as descriptive context in Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev} and never mixed with the sealed result.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\n\\subsection{Main result: the slope test is inconclusive}\nThe sealed evaluation completed with status \\texttt{qualified}. Its aggregate metrics are listed in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics}.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\nThe slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusive}: the prediction that delay decreases with width is neither supported nor refuted. A point estimate of $-0.020$ corresponds to a fitted multiplicative change of $0.980$ in delay per doubling of width, and the interval to factors between $0.557$ and $1.425$; over the full $16\\to256$ range the fitted factors are $0.924$ with interval $[0.096, 4.12]$.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#per_doubling_factor]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#range_factor_256_vs_16]]\nThat is, the data are compatible with a delay roughly $10$ times shorter at width $256$ than at $16$, with no change, and with a delay about $4$ times longer.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final evaluation: aggregate metrics (four seeds $\\times$ five widths $=20$ runs, plus two controls). ``Score'' is the mean over seeds and widths of the area under the held-out-accuracy curve over the $40$ checkpoints; it does not use the $0.99$ threshold.}\n\\label{tab:metrics}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nQuantity & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nSlope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ (point) & $-0.020$ \\\\\n95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval & $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$ \\\\\nVerdict under pre-declared rule & inconclusive \\\\\nReference-trainer slope (seed 201 only, no interval) & $-0.368$ \\\\\nCensored fraction of the 20 runs & $0.40$ ($8$ runs) \\\\\nControl maximum held-out accuracy (ceiling $0.10$) & $0.012$ \\\\\nMissing episodes & $0$ \\\\\nScore (mean held-out-accuracy AUC) & $0.729$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.92\\textwidth]{figures/slope_interval_vs_decision_rule.pdf}\n\\caption{The estimated slope and its 95\\% interval against the three decision regions. The interval straddles zero, so the verdict is inconclusive. The single-seed reference slope lies inside the interval. Dotted lines mark the largest slope magnitude attainable with delays confined to $[250, 10{,}000]$ over four width doublings.}\n\\label{fig:slope}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Resolution of the design}\n\\label{sec:resolution}\nAn inconclusive verdict is informative only relative to the effects the design could have resolved. Three properties of the protocol, all computed from its constants and the reported interval (Table~\\ref{tab:resolution}), limit that resolution.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json#full]]\n\n\\emph{Bounded dynamic range.} With delay between $250$ and $10{,}000$ steps and $\\log_2(256/16)=4$ doublings, no slope can exceed $\\ln(40)/4=0.922$ in magnitude. The interval has width $0.939$ and therefore covers $51\\%$ of the attainable range $[-0.922, 0.922]$. Its lower arm extends $0.565$ below the point estimate and its upper arm $0.374$ above.\n\n\\emph{Distance to a supported verdict.} Keeping the upper arm of the interval unchanged, a verdict of supported would have required a point estimate below $-0.374$. The reference slope ($-0.368$) is slightly above that value; with the same upper arm it would give an upper endpoint of $+0.006$, still straddling zero. This is arithmetic on the observed spread, not a forecast: the spread of a different point estimate would differ.\n\n\\emph{A coarse bootstrap.} With four seeds there are only $\\binom{7}{4}=35$ distinct multisets of seeds to resample, and a fraction $4/4^4=1/64$ of draws consist of a single seed repeated four times. The $2{,}000$ resamples therefore estimate a discrete distribution with at most $35$ support points; the percentile endpoints $-0.585$ and $0.354$ should be read as coarse, and the coverage of an interval built from four clusters is not guaranteed to be nominal.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Quantities derived from the protocol constants and the reported interval (not additional measurements).}\n\\label{tab:resolution}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nQuantity & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nDelay floor / cap (steps) & $250$ / $10{,}000$ \\\\\nLargest attainable $|\\text{slope}|$ & $0.922$ \\\\\nInterval width & $0.939$ \\\\\nInterval width as fraction of $[-0.922, 0.922]$ & $0.51$ \\\\\nInterval arms below / above the point estimate & $0.565$ / $0.374$ \\\\\nPer-doubling delay factor: point [interval] & $0.980$ [$0.557$, $1.425$] \\\\\n$16\\to256$ delay factor: point [interval] & $0.924$ [$0.096$, $4.12$] \\\\\n$16\\to256$ delay factor at the reference slope & $0.230$ \\\\\nDistinct bootstrap seed multisets & $35$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Censoring}\nForty percent of the $20$ runs ($8$ runs) were censored at the step limit and entered the regression with the cap value $10{,}000$. Censoring has two consequences. First, because censored delays are replaced by the largest permitted value, any real dependence whose long-delay end is censored is compressed, so the fitted slope is biased toward zero when long delays concentrate at one end of the width range. Second, \\emph{censored} here covers two different events, held-out accuracy never reaching $0.99$ and training accuracy never reaching $0.99$; the retained artifacts do not say which occurred or at which widths, so they cannot separate delayed generalization from failure to fit. The score in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics} offers a threshold-free view: averaged over widths and seeds, held-out accuracy integrated over the $40$ checkpoints is $0.729$, so the runs on average generalize to a substantial degree within the window, but this aggregate does not locate where in the width range that happens.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.censored_fraction]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#n_censored_runs]]\n\n\\subsection{Seed-level variability}\nThe four seeds differ considerably in the score (Table~\\ref{tab:seeds}), from $0.607$ to $0.860$ with mean $0.729$ and sample standard deviation $0.104$. We report this as variability across seeds, not as a performance comparison: each seed's score averages five widths, and a single seed supplies one run at every width. That the seed-to-seed spread is large relative to the quantities of interest is consistent with the wide interval for the slope, although the artifacts do not allow a variance decomposition across widths.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#seed_score_sd]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Seed-level records from the sealed evaluation. Score: mean over the five widths of the held-out-accuracy AUC. Wall-clock time is summed over all episodes with that seed; seed 201 also includes the two control runs.}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\nSeed & Score & Wall-clock (s) \\\\\n\\midrule\n201 & $0.607$ & $2{,}468$ \\\\\n202 & $0.733$ & $1{,}894$ \\\\\n203 & $0.717$ & $2{,}062$ \\\\\n204 & $0.860$ & $1{,}943$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean $\\pm$ s.d. & $0.729 \\pm 0.104$ & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Cross-check against the reference trainer}\nThe reference trainer, run on seed $201$ only, gives a slope of $-0.368$, a fitted $16\\to256$ delay factor of $0.230$, and lies inside the interval, $23\\%$ of the way from its lower endpoint to its upper. The evaluated model's point estimate is $0.348$ larger (less negative). We draw no conclusion about this difference: the reference slope is one seed with no uncertainty, five points define it, and the evaluated model differs from the reference in three simultaneous ways (Section~\\ref{sec:model}). The comparison also does not establish that the multipliers weaken the width dependence: that would require both variants to be run with several seeds under the same protocol, which was not done.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.reference_delay_log_slope]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#reference_position_in_ci_fraction_from_low]]\n\n\\subsection{Validity checks and development-plane context}\nThe two controls stayed far below their ceiling (maximum held-out accuracy $0.012$ against a ceiling of $0.10$), no episodes were missing, and the evaluator reports a completed, valid evaluation. These checks support the integrity of the measurement. They do not bear on the width question, and a valid measurement can be inconclusive.\n\nFor context, three configurations that differ cumulatively by the three modifications of Section~\\ref{sec:model} were evaluated on the separate development plane with two seeds each, with scores $0.798$, $0.828$, and $0.850$ and $4$, $6$, and $4$ of $6$ runs censored (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}). Differences between them are of the same size as the spread between the two seeds of any single configuration, the plane differs from the sealed one in modulus, training fraction, widths, step limit, and seeds, and no slope interval was computed; we therefore draw no inference from them.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#search]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\paragraph{Alternative readings that the data cannot separate.} The interval is compatible with four readings. (a) \\emph{Little or no width dependence} over $16$--$256$ in this setting. (b) \\emph{A real negative dependence masked by censoring and noise}: cap-compressed delays and four seeds could produce a flat-looking slope. (c) \\emph{Compensation by the width-aware multipliers}: if the hidden and output multipliers equalize effective step sizes across width, a dependence present in the stock model could be reduced in the evaluated one; the single-seed reference slope ($-0.368$) is in the direction of this reading, but it is one seed. (d) \\emph{Dependence that is non-monotone in $\\ln d$}, which a linear slope cannot register. Each reading predicts something different about per-width delays and censoring flags, which are exactly the quantities not retained.\n\n\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap, or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (a sweep script for the exponent exists but was not executed); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} The evidence concerns one operation, one modulus ($97$), one training fraction ($0.4$), one optimizer setting, a single layer, and widths $16$--$256$ (up to $0.84\\times 10^6$ parameters). The evaluated model contains three simultaneous modifications relative to the stock model, so even a clear slope would not be attributable to any one of them; sequential changes made during development support at most comparisons between whole configurations. The reference slope has one seed. The seed-cluster interval rests on four seeds. Censoring merges two events. The development-plane numbers come from a different workload with two seeds. We did not test the explanations in Section~\\ref{sec:related}, did not run any weight-norm, circuit-level, or lazy-to-rich diagnostics, and did not run ablations of the three modifications. A valid evaluation does not imply scientific support for the prediction; the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}, and the design's limits above, rather than a demonstrated absence of effect, are the most defensible reading.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\nWe tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. The sealed evaluation was valid and gave a slope of $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$ over widths $16$--$256$ and four seeds, so the prediction is neither supported nor refuted. Forty percent of runs were censored at the step limit, the interval covers about half of the slopes that the delay floor and cap allow, and a four-cluster bootstrap has only $35$ distinct resamples. A single-seed reference trainer gave $-0.368$, inside the interval. We therefore do not claim that delay depends on width, nor that it does not, and we do not attribute the difference between the reference and the evaluated model to any of its modifications. The practical lesson is a measurement one: for a delay statistic with a floor and a censoring cap, report per-width delays and censoring flags, use more seeds than clusters needed for a smooth bootstrap, and treat the attainable slope range as part of the result. The per-width records, the isolating ablation of the multipliers, and sweeps over training fraction and weight decay would turn this inconclusive result into a testable one.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Protocol and hyperparameters}\n\\label{sec:si-protocol}\nTable~\\ref{tab:si-protocol} lists the workloads of the sealed evaluation and of the development evaluation, as specified in the evaluator's workload manifests (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/workload/}).\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/search_manifest.json]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Workloads. The evaluator owns all items; the evaluated model receives only the training rows.}\n\\label{tab:si-protocol}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lll}\n\\toprule\n & Sealed final evaluation & Development evaluation \\\\\n\\midrule\nOperation, modulus $p$ & addition, $97$ & addition, $53$ \\\\\nTraining fraction & $0.4$ ($3{,}763$ pairs) & $0.5$ \\\\\nWidths $d$ & $16,32,64,128,256$ & $32,64,128$ \\\\\nSeeds & $201$--$204$ & $101,102$ \\\\\nStep limit / probe interval & $10{,}000$ / $250$ & $6{,}000$ / $300$ \\\\\nControl width & $64$ & $64$ \\\\\nOptimizer & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{AdamW, lr $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, weight decay $1.0$, full batch} \\\\\nHeads, layers & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$4$, $1$} \\\\\nFit / generalization threshold & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$0.99$ accuracy} \\\\\nBootstrap (final only) & $2{,}000$ seed-cluster resamples & -- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe final evaluation made $1{,}804$ calls to the evaluated model (limit $2{,}200$; $22$ episodes, each a reset, a data load, and $40$ train/predict pairs) and took $11{,}764$\\,s of wall-clock time including the reference trainer, against a limit of $43{,}200$\\,s.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#compute]] @evidence[[source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json#compute_usage]]\n\n\\subsection{Evaluated model: constants and parameter counts}\n\\label{sec:si-model}\nTable~\\ref{tab:si-model} gives the width-dependent constants and parameter counts of the evaluated model. The parameter count follows from the layer shapes in the source: embeddings $(p+1)d$, positional embedding $3d$, attention projections $3d^2+d^2$ (no bias), MLP $4d^2+4d$ and $4d^2+d$, unembedding $pd$ (no bias), totalling $12d^2+(2p+9)d$. The initial-weight scale ($0.5$), the unembedding scale ($0.5$), the reference width ($128$), and both exponents ($0.5$) are constants in the source; the code comments state they were chosen without offline tuning.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#params_by_width]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#hidden_and_output_multipliers]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Parameter count and multiplier $m_d=(128/d)^{1/2}$ by width.}\n\\label{tab:si-model}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{rrr}\n\\toprule\nWidth $d$ & Parameters & $m_d$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n16 & 6{,}320 & 2.83 \\\\\n32 & 18{,}784 & 2.00 \\\\\n64 & 62{,}144 & 1.41 \\\\\n128 & 222{,}592 & 1.00 \\\\\n256 & 838{,}400 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Development-plane comparison (descriptive, not final)}\n\\label{sec:si-dev}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:dev} shows the three development configurations: V1 (smaller initial weights and last-position-only computation), V2 (V1 plus width-aware output scaling), and V3 (V2 plus hidden-layer reparametrization; the evaluated model). Scores are means over two seeds on the development plane; per-seed scores are plotted. Table~\\ref{tab:si-dev} adds the mean final held-out accuracy and the censoring counts. V2 has the highest final-accuracy rank order reversed relative to the score ordering: the mean final held-out accuracy decreases from V1 to V3 ($0.981$, $0.944$, $0.921$) while the score increases ($0.798$, $0.828$, $0.850$), which shows that the score and the end-of-training accuracy measure different things. All six V2 runs are censored although their mean final held-out accuracy is $0.944$, illustrating that the $0.99$ threshold, not failure to learn, can produce censoring. With two seeds per configuration and per-seed scores of V3 ($0.801$, $0.899$) spanning the other two means, no ordering of the configurations is supported.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#search]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant1_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant2_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant3_metrics.json]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variants_descriptive.pdf}\n\\caption{Development-plane comparison of three configurations (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, two seeds; not the sealed evaluation). (a) Per-seed and mean held-out-accuracy AUC. (b) Number of the six runs censored at the step limit.}\n\\label{fig:dev}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-plane summary (two seeds $\\times$ three widths $=6$ runs per configuration).}\n\\label{tab:si-dev}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score & Mean final held-out acc. & Censored runs & Control max acc. \\\\\n\\midrule\nV1 & $0.798$ & $0.981$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\nV2 & $0.828$ & $0.944$ & $6/6$ & $0.022$ \\\\\nV3 (evaluated) & $0.850$ & $0.921$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Analyses and hypotheses not performed}\n\\label{sec:si-untested}\nThe following were considered during development and have \\emph{no} results in the artifacts: a capacity-crossover account in which fitting time falls faster with width than generalization time (separate fitting and generalization steps are not retained); a critical width as a function of training fraction; a sweep of the hidden-layer exponent (a script exists but was not run); a sweep of the output-scaling exponent and unembedding scale; an intervention on weight decay; and any weight-norm or circuit-level diagnostics. Nothing in the main text depends on them.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n\\label{sec:si-repro}\nThe accompanying files are: the evaluated model's source and the two evaluators (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/}); the aggregate and per-seed final records (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/final/}); \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}, which computes every derived number in Tables~\\ref{tab:resolution} and~\\ref{tab:si-model} and the text from the evaluator records and writes \\texttt{derived/resolution.json}; and \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py}, which produces Figures~\\ref{fig:slope} and~\\ref{fig:dev} from that file. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data. Data are generated in memory from the modulus and seed, with no download. The sealed evaluation was run once; the aggregate metrics (slope, interval, reference slope, censored fraction, control, score) are those recorded by the evaluator. Re-running the evaluator requires the evaluated model's dependencies (PyTorch and NumPy); the sealed evaluation took about $3.3$ hours of wall-clock time on the recorded hardware, whose specification is not retained in the artifacts.\n\n\\subsection{LLM usage}\n\\label{sec:si-llm}\nLarge language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators, the workload manifests, and the decision rule for the width-slope test; the models did not edit them. No results were produced by an LLM other than by executing the code described above, and all numbers in the paper come from the evaluator records or from the derivations in \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}.\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1372,"cache_read_input_tokens":159215,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1372},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dd35f6f-91b7-4850-b942-7d959e7db0b3","uuid":"98321de9-3269-4d03-ac98-af534211b352","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:18:52.851Z","request_id":"req_011CgwHRUyawLDUhYuTZmX5m"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01R7UMwVXZaCQrBF4k1hp9NU","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /workspace/paper/paper.tex (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"2dd35f6f-91b7-4850-b942-7d959e7db0b3","uuid":"3b52efb0-17bf-4ae5-b824-5d952522724a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:18:52.872Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/workspace/paper/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n% --- watermark (auto-inserted) ---\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{xfp}% \\fpeval; in the kernel since 2022, harmless on older LaTeX\n\\usepackage{draftwatermark}\n\\newlength{\\watermarkTextWidth}\n\\AddToHook{begindocument/end}{%\n  \\SetWatermarkText{\\normalfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\SetWatermarkFontSize{10pt}%\n  \\SetWatermarkLightness{0.85}%\n  \\settowidth{\\watermarkTextWidth}{\\normalfont\\fontsize{10pt}{12pt}\\selectfont Generated by The Agentic Scientist}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkScale{\\fpeval{0.8 * sqrt(\\paperwidth**2 + \\paperheight**2) / \\watermarkTextWidth}}%\n  \\edef\\watermarkAngle{\\fpeval{atand(\\paperheight/\\paperwidth)}}%\n  \\SetWatermarkScale{\\watermarkScale}%\n  \\SetWatermarkAngle{\\watermarkAngle}%\n}\n% --- end watermark ---\n % For LaTeX2e\n\\usepackage{paper,times}\n\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{subcaption}\n\n\\newcommand{\\ln}{\\operatorname{ln}}\n\n\\title{Does Grokking Delay Depend on Width? An Inconclusive, Censoring-Aware Slope Test on Modular Addition}\n\\author{Anonymous authors}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nDelayed generalization (``grokking'') on modular arithmetic is explained through memorization capacity, weight norm, circuit efficiency, or a lazy-to-rich transition, but its dependence on model size has received little systematic measurement.\nWe test one falsifiable prediction in a one-layer transformer trained with AdamW (weight decay $1.0$, full batch) on modular addition modulo $97$: that the delay between fitting the training pairs and generalizing to held-out pairs shrinks as width grows.\nDelay is right-censored at $10{,}000$ steps, and a pre-declared rule reads the 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval of the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2(\\text{width})$ over widths $16$--$256$ with four seeds.\nThe measured slope is $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$, so the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}; $40\\%$ of the $20$ runs were censored, and the interval covers about half of the slopes that the delay floor and cap permit.\nA single-seed reference trainer gives $-0.368$, inside the interval.\nThe evaluation itself was valid, so the result is a calibrated non-answer rather than a failure; we state what it cannot say and which measurements would resolve it.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\nIn small algorithmic tasks such as modular addition, networks often fit the training split almost immediately and generalize to held-out pairs only after much longer training \\citep{Power2022GrokkingGB}. Several accounts of this delay have been proposed: a mechanistic account in which a generalizing circuit forms gradually under weight decay \\citep{Nanda2023ProgressMF}, a loss-landscape and weight-norm account \\citep{Liu2022OmnigrokGB}, a circuit-efficiency account \\citep{Varma2023ExplainingGT}, and a lazy-to-rich account of training dynamics \\citep{Kumar2023GrokkingAT}. How the delay depends on \\emph{model size} is a more practical question: it constrains these accounts and informs which model sizes are worth using in experiments on the phenomenon. A capacity-based reading predicts that wider models memorize sooner, and, if generalization time is less sensitive to width, that the delay changes systematically with width.\n\nThis paper reports a single, carefully scoped measurement of that kind. The setting is a one-layer transformer trained on modular addition modulo $p=97$ with a fixed training fraction, optimizer, and step limit, and the width $d$ is varied over $\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$. The prediction under test is that the delay decreases with width, operationalized as the slope of $\\ln(\\text{delay})$ against $\\log_2 d$ being negative with a 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval entirely below zero. The protocol declares in advance that the prediction is \\emph{refuted} if the interval lies at or above zero and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero (Figure~\\ref{fig:overview}).\n\nThe outcome is inconclusive. The sealed evaluation, run on four seeds at each of five widths, gives a slope of $-0.020$ with 95\\% interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$. The evaluation was valid in the sense that it completed and passed the evaluator's validity checks (the evaluator labels it \\texttt{qualified}); this status concerns the integrity of the measurement and does not mean that the width prediction was supported. Because a flat answer is easy to misread, much of this paper is about \\emph{why} the measurement is inconclusive and how large an effect it could have resolved.\n\n\\paragraph{Contributions.} (i) We state precisely the delay statistic, its floor and censoring cap, the seed-cluster slope test, and the three-way decision rule that the sealed evaluation uses (Section~\\ref{sec:method}). (ii) We report the measured outcome with its uncertainty and its cross-check against a single-seed reference trainer (Section~\\ref{sec:results}). (iii) We derive, from the protocol constants and the reported interval, the resolution limits of this design: the range of slopes the floor and cap allow, the fraction of that range covered by the interval, and the coarseness of a four-cluster bootstrap (Section~\\ref{sec:resolution}). (iv) We list what the retained artifacts cannot support, including per-width delays, a mechanistic decomposition, and any isolation of the evaluated model's width-dependent parametrization (Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}). We make no claim that delay is, or is not, width-dependent in modular addition.\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/protocol_overview.png}\n\\caption{Overview of the width-dependence test. Delay is the gap between the first checkpoint at which training accuracy reaches $0.99$ and the first at which held-out accuracy does, floored at one probe interval and set to the step limit if either threshold is never reached. The slope of $\\ln$(delay) against $\\log_2$(width) is estimated over five widths and four seeds, with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval; the interval, not the point estimate, determines the verdict. In this study the interval straddles zero and the verdict is inconclusive.}\n\\label{fig:overview}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\\paragraph{Grokking and its explanations.} \\citet{Power2022GrokkingGB} report that small transformers trained on algorithmically generated tables, including modular arithmetic, reach high held-out accuracy long after fitting the training split. \\citet{Nanda2023ProgressMF} reverse-engineer one-layer transformers trained on modular addition and define progress measures that track the formation of the generalizing circuit during training. \\citet{Liu2022OmnigrokGB} analyze loss landscapes and weight norm and extend the phenomenon beyond algorithmic data; \\citet{Varma2023ExplainingGT} explain grokking through circuit efficiency and test predictions of that account in training experiments; \\citet{Kumar2023GrokkingAT} relate it to a transition from lazy to rich training dynamics, analyzed in a two-layer regression setting; \\citet{Huang2024UnifiedVO} discuss grokking, double descent, and emergent abilities through the competition of circuits; and \\citet{Davies2023UnifyingGA} place grokking and double descent in one framework. These accounts differ in which quantity should control the delay, and several involve scale-related knobs. The present study does not test them against each other.\n\n\\paragraph{Model size, memorization, and double descent.} Test error can vary non-monotonically with model size and training time \\citep{Nakkiran2019DeepDD}; networks can fit random labels, so fitting the training set does not by itself imply generalization \\citep{Zhang2016UnderstandingDL}; memorization has been studied empirically during language-model training \\citep{Tirumala2022MemorizationWO} and its information content estimated per model \\citep{Morris2025HowMD}. These works motivate a capacity-based reading of the grokking delay, in which fitting time and generalization time depend differently on width. We use them only as motivation: the artifacts of this study do not record fitting time and generalization time separately.\n\n\\paragraph{Parametrization and optimizer scale.} The evaluated model contains width-dependent multipliers on hidden and output layers (Section~\\ref{sec:model}), motivated by the observation that the step size a layer effectively receives under an adaptive optimizer depends on its parametrization. The role of effective learning rates under normalization has been studied in reinforcement learning \\citep{Lyle2024NormalizationAE}, practical measures of dynamical richness have been proposed \\citep{Nam2024DecouplingDR}, and decoupled weight decay, which we use, is introduced by \\citet{Loshchilov2017DecoupledWD}. We do not measure the effect of these multipliers in isolation.\n\n\\paragraph{Relation to this work.} Rather than propose a new explanation, we examine what a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test on one architecture and one task can and cannot resolve, and we report an inconclusive result in full.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Problem setup and hypothesis}\n\\label{sec:setup}\n\\paragraph{Task.} Inputs are all $p^2=9{,}409$ ordered pairs $(a,b)$ with $a,b\\in\\{0,\\dots,96\\}$ and label $(a+b)\\bmod 97$. For each seed, a random $40\\%$ of pairs ($3{,}763$) forms the training split and the remaining $5{,}646$ pairs are held out; the evaluator, not the model, owns the split, the checkpoint schedule, and all metrics.\n\n\\paragraph{Delay.} Training runs for at most $T=10{,}000$ full-batch steps and is probed every $250$ steps, giving $40$ checkpoints at which accuracy is computed by the evaluator on all pairs. Let $t_{\\mathrm{fit}}$ be the first checkpoint at which training accuracy is at least $0.99$, and $t_{\\mathrm{gen}}$ the first at which held-out accuracy is at least $0.99$. The delay is\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta = \\begin{cases} \\max(t_{\\mathrm{gen}}-t_{\\mathrm{fit}},\\,250) & \\text{if both thresholds are reached,}\\\\ 10{,}000 & \\text{otherwise (censored).}\\end{cases}\n\\end{equation}\nA run is censored if \\emph{either} threshold is never reached. Censored runs keep the cap value and are never dropped or reweighted. The floor of one probe interval ($250$ steps) also applies when held-out accuracy crosses $0.99$ no later than training accuracy does. Both the floor and the cap therefore bound $\\ln\\Delta$ to $[\\ln 250,\\ln 10{,}000]$, a ratio of $40$.\n\n\\paragraph{Test statistic and decision rule.} With widths $d\\in\\{16,32,64,128,256\\}$ and seeds $s\\in\\{201,202,203,204\\}$ the statistic is the ordinary-least-squares slope $\\beta$ of $\\ln\\Delta_{d,s}$ on $\\log_2 d$ over the $20$ pairs. Its interval is the 2.5--97.5 percentile range of $2{,}000$ bootstrap slopes in which seeds are resampled with replacement and each resampled seed contributes all five of its widths (a cluster bootstrap over seeds, with a fixed resampling seed). The decision rule is declared before the final evaluation: the prediction ``delay decreases with width'' is \\emph{supported} if the interval lies entirely below zero, \\emph{refuted} if it lies at or above zero, and \\emph{inconclusive} if it straddles zero or the evidence is incomplete. The claim is restricted to the tested setting (modular addition, one training fraction, AdamW with weight decay $1.0$, full batch, one layer, the five widths) and makes no statement about other operations, moduli, optimizers, depths, or larger models.\n\n\\subsection{Evaluated model}\n\\label{sec:model}\nThe evaluated configuration is a one-layer, four-head transformer with width $d$ (the model dimension), an MLP of hidden size $4d$ with ReLU, no normalization layers, a learned embedding for the $97$ residues plus a separator token ``$=$'' appended as the third input position, and a learned positional embedding. Attention is non-causal and only the representation at the final position is read out; the implementation computes queries, residual stream, and MLP for that position only, which by inspection of the one-layer architecture gives the same outputs as processing all positions. The parameter count is $12d^2+(2p+9)d$, from $6{,}320$ at $d=16$ to $838{,}400$ at $d=256$, within the $10^6$ budget (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-model}).\n\nRelative to the evaluator's stock model, the configuration has three modifications, all visible in the source (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/frozen\\_model/program.py}):\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\emph{Smaller initial weights.} All hidden and output weight matrices are multiplied by $0.5$ at initialization.\n\\item \\emph{Width-aware output scaling.} The unembedding weights are additionally halved at initialization, and the logits are multiplied by $m_d=(128/d)^{1/2}$.\n\\item \\emph{Hidden-layer reparametrization.} Each hidden matrix is written $W=m_d V$ with the same multiplier $m_d$, and $V$ is initialized as $W_{\\mathrm{init}}/m_d$ so that the function at initialization is unchanged. The optimizer updates $V$, and decoupled weight decay acts on $V$. The code comments state the intent: under Adam-type updates $V$ moves at about the learning rate per step, so $W$ moves at about $m_d$ times the learning rate, which is meant to compensate for width in the effective step size.\n\\end{enumerate}\nThe multiplier is $2.83, 2.0, 1.41, 1.0, 0.71$ at $d=16,\\dots,256$. The exponents ($0.5$) and the reference width ($128$) are fixed constants chosen before the final evaluation, not tuned on it. The training workload is dictated by the evaluator: AdamW with learning rate $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, decoupled weight decay $1.0$, full-batch updates on all $3{,}763$ training pairs, and $10{,}000$ steps. We refer to this configuration as the \\emph{evaluated model}.\n\n\\subsection{Reference trainer, controls, and validity}\n\\label{sec:controls}\n\\paragraph{Reference trainer.} The evaluator also trains its own stock transformer with the same optimizer settings and no modifications (no initialization shrinkage, no multipliers, all positions processed), using the first final seed ($201$) at each of the five widths. The OLS slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ over these five single-seed delays is the reference slope. It has no interval.\n\n\\paragraph{Controls.} Two control runs at width $64$ and seed $201$ check that the measurement does not manufacture generalization: one trains on training labels that have been permuted (at least $90\\%$ of labels changed) and one performs zero optimizer steps. A control fails if its held-out accuracy ever exceeds $0.10$; chance level for $97$ classes is about $0.010$.\n\n\\paragraph{Validity.} The evaluator marks the final evaluation valid (\\texttt{qualified}, status \\texttt{completed}) when, according to its source, all episodes completed, the compute and call budgets were respected, the controls passed, and at least one run fit the training data to $0.99$. Only these three status fields are retained in the artifacts; per-condition diagnostics beyond them are not, so we treat any finer breakdown as unknown. Diagnostics reported by the evaluated model itself are untrusted and not used: every accuracy, delay, control, and statistic below is computed by the evaluator from returned predictions.\n\n\\subsection{Scope of the retained evidence}\n\\label{sec:scope}\nThe retained final artifacts consist of the aggregate metrics listed in Section~\\ref{sec:results} and one record per seed (mean validation-accuracy score and wall-clock time). \\emph{Per-width and per-seed delays, fitting and generalization steps, and per-width censoring flags are not retained}, so we cannot show a delay-versus-width plot, and we do not infer one. Separately, a development evaluation on a different data plane (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, widths $32,64,128$, $6{,}000$ steps, two seeds) was used when configuring the evaluated model; its results are reported only as descriptive context in Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev} and never mixed with the sealed result.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\label{sec:results}\n\n\\subsection{Main result: the slope test is inconclusive}\nThe sealed evaluation completed with status \\texttt{qualified}. Its aggregate metrics are listed in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics}.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\nThe slope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ is $-0.020$, with 95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$. Because the interval contains zero, the pre-declared rule returns \\emph{inconclusive}: the prediction that delay decreases with width is neither supported nor refuted. A point estimate of $-0.020$ corresponds to a fitted multiplicative change of $0.980$ in delay per doubling of width, and the interval to factors between $0.557$ and $1.425$; over the full $16\\to256$ range the fitted factors are $0.924$ with interval $[0.096, 4.12]$.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#per_doubling_factor]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#range_factor_256_vs_16]]\nThat is, the data are compatible with a delay roughly $10$ times shorter at width $256$ than at $16$, with no change, and with a delay about $4$ times longer.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Sealed final evaluation: aggregate metrics (four seeds $\\times$ five widths $=20$ runs, plus two controls). ``Score'' is the mean over seeds and widths of the area under the held-out-accuracy curve over the $40$ checkpoints; it does not use the $0.99$ threshold.}\n\\label{tab:metrics}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nQuantity & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nSlope of $\\ln\\Delta$ on $\\log_2 d$ (point) & $-0.020$ \\\\\n95\\% seed-cluster bootstrap interval & $[-0.585,\\,0.354]$ \\\\\nVerdict under pre-declared rule & inconclusive \\\\\nReference-trainer slope (seed 201 only, no interval) & $-0.368$ \\\\\nCensored fraction of the 20 runs & $0.40$ ($8$ runs) \\\\\nControl maximum held-out accuracy (ceiling $0.10$) & $0.012$ \\\\\nMissing episodes & $0$ \\\\\nScore (mean held-out-accuracy AUC) & $0.729$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=0.92\\textwidth]{figures/slope_interval_vs_decision_rule.pdf}\n\\caption{The estimated slope and its 95\\% interval against the three decision regions. The interval straddles zero, so the verdict is inconclusive. The single-seed reference slope lies inside the interval. Dotted lines mark the largest slope magnitude attainable with delays confined to $[250, 10{,}000]$ over four width doublings.}\n\\label{fig:slope}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Resolution of the design}\n\\label{sec:resolution}\nAn inconclusive verdict is informative only relative to the effects the design could have resolved. Three properties of the protocol, all computed from its constants and the reported interval (Table~\\ref{tab:resolution}), limit that resolution.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json#full]]\n\n\\emph{Bounded dynamic range.} With delay between $250$ and $10{,}000$ steps and $\\log_2(256/16)=4$ doublings, no slope can exceed $\\ln(40)/4=0.922$ in magnitude. The interval has width $0.939$ and therefore covers $51\\%$ of the attainable range $[-0.922, 0.922]$. Its lower arm extends $0.565$ below the point estimate and its upper arm $0.374$ above.\n\n\\emph{Distance to a supported verdict.} Keeping the upper arm of the interval unchanged, a verdict of supported would have required a point estimate below $-0.374$. The reference slope ($-0.368$) is slightly above that value; with the same upper arm it would give an upper endpoint of $+0.006$, still straddling zero. This is arithmetic on the observed spread, not a forecast: the spread of a different point estimate would differ.\n\n\\emph{A coarse bootstrap.} With four seeds there are only $\\binom{7}{4}=35$ distinct multisets of seeds to resample, and a fraction $4/4^4=1/64$ of draws consist of a single seed repeated four times. The $2{,}000$ resamples therefore estimate a discrete distribution with at most $35$ support points; the percentile endpoints $-0.585$ and $0.354$ should be read as coarse, and the coverage of an interval built from four clusters is not guaranteed to be nominal.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Quantities derived from the protocol constants and the reported interval (not additional measurements).}\n\\label{tab:resolution}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lr}\n\\toprule\nQuantity & Value \\\\\n\\midrule\nDelay floor / cap (steps) & $250$ / $10{,}000$ \\\\\nLargest attainable $|\\text{slope}|$ & $0.922$ \\\\\nInterval width & $0.939$ \\\\\nInterval width as fraction of $[-0.922, 0.922]$ & $0.51$ \\\\\nInterval arms below / above the point estimate & $0.565$ / $0.374$ \\\\\nPer-doubling delay factor: point [interval] & $0.980$ [$0.557$, $1.425$] \\\\\n$16\\to256$ delay factor: point [interval] & $0.924$ [$0.096$, $4.12$] \\\\\n$16\\to256$ delay factor at the reference slope & $0.230$ \\\\\nDistinct bootstrap seed multisets & $35$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Censoring}\nForty percent of the $20$ runs ($8$ runs) were censored at the step limit and entered the regression with the cap value $10{,}000$. Censoring has two consequences. First, because censored delays are replaced by the largest permitted value, any real dependence whose long-delay end is censored is compressed, so the fitted slope is biased toward zero when long delays concentrate at one end of the width range. Second, \\emph{censored} here covers two different events, held-out accuracy never reaching $0.99$ and training accuracy never reaching $0.99$; the retained artifacts do not say which occurred or at which widths, so they cannot separate delayed generalization from failure to fit. The score in Table~\\ref{tab:metrics} offers a threshold-free view: averaged over widths and seeds, held-out accuracy integrated over the $40$ checkpoints is $0.729$, so the runs on average generalize to a substantial degree within the window, but this aggregate does not locate where in the width range that happens.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.censored_fraction]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#n_censored_runs]]\n\n\\subsection{Seed-level variability}\nThe four seeds differ considerably in the score (Table~\\ref{tab:seeds}), from $0.607$ to $0.860$ with mean $0.729$ and sample standard deviation $0.104$. We report this as variability across seeds, not as a performance comparison: each seed's score averages five widths, and a single seed supplies one run at every width. That the seed-to-seed spread is large relative to the quantities of interest is consistent with the wide interval for the slope, although the artifacts do not allow a variance decomposition across widths.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#seed_score_sd]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Seed-level records from the sealed evaluation. Score: mean over the five widths of the held-out-accuracy AUC. Wall-clock time is summed over all episodes with that seed; seed 201 also includes the two control runs.}\n\\label{tab:seeds}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\nSeed & Score & Wall-clock (s) \\\\\n\\midrule\n201 & $0.607$ & $2{,}468$ \\\\\n202 & $0.733$ & $1{,}894$ \\\\\n203 & $0.717$ & $2{,}062$ \\\\\n204 & $0.860$ & $1{,}943$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nMean $\\pm$ s.d. & $0.729 \\pm 0.104$ & \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.evaluation_evidence.episodes]]\n\n\\subsection{Cross-check against the reference trainer}\nThe reference trainer, run on seed $201$ only, gives a slope of $-0.368$, a fitted $16\\to256$ delay factor of $0.230$, and lies inside the interval, $23\\%$ of the way from its lower endpoint to its upper. The evaluated model's point estimate is $0.348$ larger (less negative). We draw no conclusion about this difference: the reference slope is one seed with no uncertainty, five points define it, and the evaluated model differs from the reference in three simultaneous ways (Section~\\ref{sec:model}). The comparison also does not establish that the multipliers weaken the width dependence: that would require both variants to be run with several seeds under the same protocol, which was not done.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.reference_delay_log_slope]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#reference_position_in_ci_fraction_from_low]]\n\n\\subsection{Validity checks and development-plane context}\nThe two controls stayed far below their ceiling (maximum held-out accuracy $0.012$ against a ceiling of $0.10$), no episodes were missing, and the evaluator reports a completed, valid evaluation. These checks support the integrity of the measurement. They do not bear on the width question, and a valid measurement can be inconclusive.\n\nFor context, three configurations that differ cumulatively by the three modifications of Section~\\ref{sec:model} were evaluated on the separate development plane with two seeds each, with scores $0.798$, $0.828$, and $0.850$ and $4$, $6$, and $4$ of $6$ runs censored (Appendix~\\ref{sec:si-dev}). Differences between them are of the same size as the spread between the two seeds of any single configuration, the plane differs from the sealed one in modulus, training fraction, widths, step limit, and seeds, and no slope interval was computed; we therefore draw no inference from them.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#search]]\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\paragraph{Alternative readings that the data cannot separate.} The interval is compatible with four readings. (a) \\emph{Little or no width dependence} over $16$--$256$ in this setting. (b) \\emph{A real negative dependence masked by censoring and noise}: cap-compressed delays and four seeds could produce a flat-looking slope. (c) \\emph{Compensation by the width-aware multipliers}: if the hidden and output multipliers equalize effective step sizes across width, a dependence present in the stock model could be reduced in the evaluated one; the single-seed reference slope ($-0.368$) is in the direction of this reading, but it is one seed. (d) \\emph{Dependence that is non-monotone in $\\ln d$}, which a linear slope cannot register. Each reading predicts something different about per-width delays and censoring flags, which are exactly the quantities not retained.\n\n\\paragraph{What would resolve it.} Without claiming these were performed: (i) report per-width, per-seed delays with explicit censoring flags and the reason for censoring (not fitting vs.\\ not generalizing); (ii) analyze delays as censored data instead of imputing the cap, or raise the step limit until few runs are censored; (iii) use more seeds, so that the bootstrap has more than $35$ distinct resamples; (iv) run the multipliers' exponent at $0$ and $0.5$ under the same protocol to isolate their effect (a sweep script for the exponent exists but was not executed); (v) vary the training fraction, the modulus, and the weight decay, which determine the delay in the accounts reviewed in Section~\\ref{sec:related}; and (vi) record fitting and generalization steps separately to test the capacity-based reading directly.\n\n\\paragraph{Limitations.} The evidence concerns one operation, one modulus ($97$), one training fraction ($0.4$), one optimizer setting, a single layer, and widths $16$--$256$ (up to $0.84\\times 10^6$ parameters). The evaluated model contains three simultaneous modifications relative to the stock model, so even a clear slope would not be attributable to any one of them; sequential changes made during development support at most comparisons between whole configurations. The reference slope has one seed. The seed-cluster interval rests on four seeds. Censoring merges two events. The development-plane numbers come from a different workload with two seeds. We did not test the explanations in Section~\\ref{sec:related}, did not run any weight-norm, circuit-level, or lazy-to-rich diagnostics, and did not run ablations of the three modifications. A valid evaluation does not imply scientific support for the prediction; the verdict is \\emph{inconclusive}, and the design's limits above, rather than a demonstrated absence of effect, are the most defensible reading.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\nWe tested whether the grokking delay of a one-layer transformer on modular addition decreases with width, using a pre-declared, censoring-aware slope test with a seed-cluster bootstrap interval. The sealed evaluation was valid and gave a slope of $-0.020$ with interval $[-0.585, 0.354]$ over widths $16$--$256$ and four seeds, so the prediction is neither supported nor refuted. Forty percent of runs were censored at the step limit, the interval covers about half of the slopes that the delay floor and cap allow, and a four-cluster bootstrap has only $35$ distinct resamples. A single-seed reference trainer gave $-0.368$, inside the interval. We therefore do not claim that delay depends on width, nor that it does not, and we do not attribute the difference between the reference and the evaluated model to any of its modifications. The practical lesson is a measurement one: for a delay statistic with a floor and a censoring cap, report per-width delays and censoring flags, use more seeds than clusters needed for a smooth bootstrap, and treat the attainable slope range as part of the result. The per-width records, the isolating ablation of the multipliers, and sweeps over training fraction and weight decay would turn this inconclusive result into a testable one.\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Protocol and hyperparameters}\n\\label{sec:si-protocol}\nTable~\\ref{tab:si-protocol} lists the workloads of the sealed evaluation and of the development evaluation, as specified in the evaluator's workload manifests (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/workload/}).\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/final_manifest.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/workload/search_manifest.json]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Workloads. The evaluator owns all items; the evaluated model receives only the training rows.}\n\\label{tab:si-protocol}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lll}\n\\toprule\n & Sealed final evaluation & Development evaluation \\\\\n\\midrule\nOperation, modulus $p$ & addition, $97$ & addition, $53$ \\\\\nTraining fraction & $0.4$ ($3{,}763$ pairs) & $0.5$ \\\\\nWidths $d$ & $16,32,64,128,256$ & $32,64,128$ \\\\\nSeeds & $201$--$204$ & $101,102$ \\\\\nStep limit / probe interval & $10{,}000$ / $250$ & $6{,}000$ / $300$ \\\\\nControl width & $64$ & $64$ \\\\\nOptimizer & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{AdamW, lr $10^{-3}$, $\\beta=(0.9,0.98)$, weight decay $1.0$, full batch} \\\\\nHeads, layers & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$4$, $1$} \\\\\nFit / generalization threshold & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$0.99$ accuracy} \\\\\nBootstrap (final only) & $2{,}000$ seed-cluster resamples & -- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe final evaluation made $1{,}804$ calls to the evaluated model (limit $2{,}200$; $22$ episodes, each a reset, a data load, and $40$ train/predict pairs) and took $11{,}764$\\,s of wall-clock time including the reference trainer, against a limit of $43{,}200$\\,s.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#compute]] @evidence[[source_evidence/final/final_manifest.json#compute_usage]]\n\n\\subsection{Evaluated model: constants and parameter counts}\n\\label{sec:si-model}\nTable~\\ref{tab:si-model} gives the width-dependent constants and parameter counts of the evaluated model. The parameter count follows from the layer shapes in the source: embeddings $(p+1)d$, positional embedding $3d$, attention projections $3d^2+d^2$ (no bias), MLP $4d^2+4d$ and $4d^2+d$, unembedding $pd$ (no bias), totalling $12d^2+(2p+9)d$. The initial-weight scale ($0.5$), the unembedding scale ($0.5$), the reference width ($128$), and both exponents ($0.5$) are constants in the source; the code comments state they were chosen without offline tuning.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/frozen_model/program.py]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#params_by_width]] @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#hidden_and_output_multipliers]]\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Parameter count and multiplier $m_d=(128/d)^{1/2}$ by width.}\n\\label{tab:si-model}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{rrr}\n\\toprule\nWidth $d$ & Parameters & $m_d$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n16 & 6{,}320 & 2.83 \\\\\n32 & 18{,}784 & 2.00 \\\\\n64 & 62{,}144 & 1.41 \\\\\n128 & 222{,}592 & 1.00 \\\\\n256 & 838{,}400 & 0.71 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Development-plane comparison (descriptive, not final)}\n\\label{sec:si-dev}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:dev} shows the three development configurations: V1 (smaller initial weights and last-position-only computation), V2 (V1 plus width-aware output scaling), and V3 (V2 plus hidden-layer reparametrization; the evaluated model). Scores are means over two seeds on the development plane; per-seed scores are plotted. Table~\\ref{tab:si-dev} adds the mean final held-out accuracy and the censoring counts. V2 has the highest final-accuracy rank order reversed relative to the score ordering: the mean final held-out accuracy decreases from V1 to V3 ($0.981$, $0.944$, $0.921$) while the score increases ($0.798$, $0.828$, $0.850$), which shows that the score and the end-of-training accuracy measure different things. All six V2 runs are censored although their mean final held-out accuracy is $0.944$, illustrating that the $0.99$ threshold, not failure to learn, can produce censoring. With two seeds per configuration and per-seed scores of V3 ($0.801$, $0.899$) spanning the other two means, no ordering of the configurations is supported.\n% @evidence[[derived/resolution.json#search]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant1_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant2_metrics.json]] @evidence[[source_evidence/search_variants/variant3_metrics.json]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[h]\n\\centering\n\\includegraphics[width=\\textwidth]{figures/search_variants_descriptive.pdf}\n\\caption{Development-plane comparison of three configurations (modulus $53$, training fraction $0.5$, two seeds; not the sealed evaluation). (a) Per-seed and mean held-out-accuracy AUC. (b) Number of the six runs censored at the step limit.}\n\\label{fig:dev}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\begin{table}[h]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Development-plane summary (two seeds $\\times$ three widths $=6$ runs per configuration).}\n\\label{tab:si-dev}\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nConfiguration & Score & Mean final held-out acc. & Censored runs & Control max acc. \\\\\n\\midrule\nV1 & $0.798$ & $0.981$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\nV2 & $0.828$ & $0.944$ & $6/6$ & $0.022$ \\\\\nV3 (evaluated) & $0.850$ & $0.921$ & $4/6$ & $0.023$ \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Analyses and hypotheses not performed}\n\\label{sec:si-untested}\nThe following were considered during development and have \\emph{no} results in the artifacts: a capacity-crossover account in which fitting time falls faster with width than generalization time (separate fitting and generalization steps are not retained); a critical width as a function of training fraction; a sweep of the hidden-layer exponent (a script exists but was not run); a sweep of the output-scaling exponent and unembedding scale; an intervention on weight decay; and any weight-norm or circuit-level diagnostics. Nothing in the main text depends on them.\n\n\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n\\label{sec:si-repro}\nThe accompanying files are: the evaluated model's source and the two evaluators (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/}); the aggregate and per-seed final records (\\texttt{source\\_evidence/final/}); \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}, which computes every derived number in Tables~\\ref{tab:resolution} and~\\ref{tab:si-model} and the text from the evaluator records and writes \\texttt{derived/resolution.json}; and \\texttt{scripts/build\\_figures.py}, which produces Figures~\\ref{fig:slope} and~\\ref{fig:dev} from that file. Figure~\\ref{fig:overview} is a schematic with no data. Data are generated in memory from the modulus and seed, with no download. The sealed evaluation was run once; the aggregate metrics (slope, interval, reference slope, censored fraction, control, score) are those recorded by the evaluator. Re-running the evaluator requires the evaluated model's dependencies (PyTorch and NumPy); the sealed evaluation took about $3.3$ hours of wall-clock time on the recorded hardware, whose specification is not retained in the artifacts.\n\n\\subsection{LLM usage}\n\\label{sec:si-llm}\nLarge language models acted as autonomous agents in this work: they proposed the candidate directions, wrote and modified the evaluated model's code, drafted the analysis scripts, and wrote this manuscript. The task environment supplied the evaluators, the workload manifests, and the decision rule for the width-slope test; the models did not edit them. No results were produced by an LLM other than by executing the code described above, and all numbers in the paper come from the evaluator records or from the derivations in \\texttt{scripts/derive\\_resolution.py}.\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
